WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.760
Hulu has put out a ton of fantastic original&nbsp;
movies over the last few years — so many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.640
in fact, that it can be hard&nbsp;
to know where to start. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.200
fear not: because these are the best Hulu&nbsp;
movies you can't watch anywhere else.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.960
Bad Hair starts off pretty unassumingly. Directed&nbsp;
by Justin Simien of Dear White People fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.480
the movie follows Anna, a woman who&nbsp;
decides to get a weave in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.720
become more successful in the world&nbsp;
of music TV. So far, so normal, right?

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.640
But things get kinda weird after that, first&nbsp;
when Anna discovers that her hair is somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.160
moving on its own, and then when she begins&nbsp;
to have some very strange dreams indeed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.360
Ultimately, Anna's hair grows a life of its own,&nbsp;
and begins to terrorize the people around her.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.000
Although the premise may seem absurd, Bad&nbsp;
Hair is also a clear-eyed critique of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.920
way beauty is dictated by standards&nbsp;
that tend to be overwhelmingly white.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.440
In getting a weave, Anna lets those&nbsp;
standards take control of her life;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.000
a metaphor Simien literalizes in the text&nbsp;
of his film. This is Simien's first foray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.080
into the horror genre, and one that's&nbsp;
more than worth 90 minutes of your time.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.040
Rapper Tekashi 6ix9ine has left a&nbsp;
huge impression on popular culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.760
despite his youth — so maybe that's&nbsp;
why 69: The Saga of Danny Hernandez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.120
caused such a stir upon its initial release.&nbsp;
This documentary film explores the controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.800
rapper's life story, including his rise&nbsp;
to prominence in the world of music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.040
his continued affiliation with street&nbsp;
gangs, and his several arrests.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.520
Hernandez's story is just about crazy&nbsp;
enough to be worthy of a documentary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.080
although director Vikram Gandhi is limited&nbsp;
by the rapper's refusal to participate in or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.600
cooperate with filming. Gandhi gets plenty of&nbsp;
great footage from the people who know Hernandez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.320
best, but without speaking to the rapper&nbsp;
himself, it's impossible to get the fullest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.000
possible sense of who he is. Even so, The Saga&nbsp;
of Danny Hernandez offers up plenty of insight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.800
into its subject's growing fame, and what it&nbsp;
means for both him and the people around him.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.640
There's a good reason why hugely successful con&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.400
men are such compelling subjects&nbsp;
for documentaries. In retrospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.040
it seems obvious that the entire illusion they&nbsp;
created was built on a house of cards — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.560
only makes the fact that someone was able to&nbsp;
pull the whole thing off even more compelling.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.920
WeWork: Or the Making and Breaking&nbsp;
of a $47 Billion Unicorn provides an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.920
in-depth look at one of the biggest&nbsp;
corporate busts in American history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.200
Telling the story of the rise and fall of WeWork&nbsp;
and its ultra-charismatic CEO Adam Neumann,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.280
the film chronicles the ways in which Neumann&nbsp;
was able to convince his employees and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.960
investors that the company was worth&nbsp;
billions more than it actually was.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.560
Thanks to a number of interviews with&nbsp;
a wide range of different people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.600
this documentary is able to articulate&nbsp;
precisely just how Neumann pulled one over on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.000
everyone. Although it was criticized for&nbsp;
failing to examine the broader culture of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.840
capitalism that facilitated Neumann's&nbsp;
scam, the documentary is nonetheless a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:00.000
compelling analysis of exactly how WeWork&nbsp;
came to be — and why it all went so wrong.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.760
In Boss Level, Frank Grillo plays Roy Pulver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.040
a former soldier who is trapped in a time loop&nbsp;
of the day that invariably ends with his death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.960
Over the course of the movie, Pulver has to&nbsp;
unravel the government conspiracy that played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.280
a role in his own murder, and take down a&nbsp;
villainous colonel played by Mel Gibson.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.920
"What the hell. Let's take&nbsp;
this to the next level."

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.760
Much of what you'll find in Boss Level is exactly&nbsp;
what you'll find in any standard action movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.360
but the addition of the time loop element&nbsp;
makes all that conventional plotting feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.760
impressively fresh. What's more, Grillo&nbsp;
— usually a supporting player in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.960
kinds of action movies — takes on the lead role&nbsp;
here, and doesn't waste a second of screen time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.680
And sure, the movie may not be high art,&nbsp;
but hey, not everything needs to be.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.680
There's no shortage of stories&nbsp;
about what it's like to grow up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.640
famous. Some look at the experience as an&nbsp;
incredibly exciting and enviable adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.760
while others lament the loneliness of the&nbsp;
celeb lifestyle. But Kid 90 offers a uniquely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.560
on-the-ground account of what it's like to grow up&nbsp;
on the silver screen. The movie features archival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.360
footage taken by Soleil Moon Frye, who became&nbsp;
famous as TV's beloved Punky Brewster. As it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.400
turns out, Frye carried a video camera with her&nbsp;
everywhere she went throughout much of the '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.800
and so managed to capture all the trials&nbsp;
and tribulations of young stardom.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.840
The documentary features interviews with a number&nbsp;
of stars and celebs, including Frye herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.120
on what it was like to grow up with a famous face&nbsp;
during the '90s. Between all that archival footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.760
and all those interviews, Kid 90 paints a&nbsp;
compelling portrait of a group of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.720
who grew up in the spotlight — and had to deal&nbsp;
with the consequences for the rest of their lives.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.480
It's not often that a great actor is able to truly&nbsp;
embody an iconic role. In fact, the sad truth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.440
that sometimes a performer just isn't a good match&nbsp;
for a particular character. In Becoming Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.880
filmmaker Josh Greenbaum examines one very&nbsp;
particular era of the James Bond franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.240
in order to take a closer look at&nbsp;
the relationship between character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.320
and actor. Specifically, Greenbaum&nbsp;
focuses on the career of George Lazenby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:08.480
the Australian actor who only portrayed Bond&nbsp;
in 1969's On Her Majesty's Secret Service.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.000
"This never happened to the other fella."

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.920
The film uses re-enactments of scenes from&nbsp;
Lazenby's life, as well as actual footage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.800
interviews with the actor, to examine why he took&nbsp;
on the role of Bond, and why he eventually decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.080
to leave the role after just one film. And while&nbsp;
Lazenby's time as James Bond is an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.320
enough chapter in the character's on-screen&nbsp;
history, Becoming Bond covers far more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.800
that. It also flips the script by exploring&nbsp;
the way that the Bond character influenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.840
Lazenby's own life, and how it ultimately&nbsp;
shaped every role he took on afterwards.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.680
Thanks to her passionate efforts to&nbsp;
raise awareness about climate change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.160
Greta Thunberg has become one of the most&nbsp;
famous activists in the world. Citizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.640
of every continent have watched her speak&nbsp;
at the UN, or followed her as she traveled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.280
across the world by boat. I Am Greta,&nbsp;
however, offers a more intimate look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.080
Thunberg's efforts to convince the world that&nbsp;
the climate crisis is its number one problem.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.120
The film was knocked slightly for its lack of&nbsp;
truly revelatory insights into Thunberg herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:11.600
but it's clear from her public persona that&nbsp;
she's not really interested in hogging any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:17.040
glory. Instead, she's focused on an issue that&nbsp;
she sees as an emergency, and the documentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.920
makes it clear that her mission is much more&nbsp;
important to her than any specific details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:27.120
about her own life. Regardless of whether you&nbsp;
learn anything new about her or her cause, I Am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.080
Greta should help you understand why her activism&nbsp;
has been so transformative for so many people.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.600
Weirdly enough, what The Amazing Johnathan&nbsp;
Documentary really gets at is what it actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:42.000
means to make a documentary. The movie's director,&nbsp;
Ben Berman, follows the Amazing Johnathan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.960
a magician dying of heart disease on his final&nbsp;
tour. That somewhat simple setup may sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:52.160
conventional enough, but this film has a number&nbsp;
of twists, each of which builds upon the others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.440
until it becomes almost impossible to discern&nbsp;
what the truth being presented even is.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.360
Part of what makes The Amazing Johnathan&nbsp;
Documentary work so well is that it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:07.280
to recognize, at least in part, how foolhardy&nbsp;
it is to try and find the truth while you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:11.680
pointing a camera at someone. The movie&nbsp;
argues that truth is always illusory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.520
and that this is especially the case&nbsp;
when the subject has their own agenda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.240
With the tricks that it manages to pull off, The&nbsp;
Amazing Johnathan Documentary turns the concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.960
of truth on its head, and proves that magic&nbsp;
doesn't always have to be sleight-of-hand.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.160
Although many of Hulu's most successful&nbsp;
projects are documentaries, its narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.760
films have also enjoyed a great deal of&nbsp;
acclaim. Little Monsters, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.600
is one of the streaming service's most&nbsp;
popular movies, thanks in large part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.160
to a hugely memorable performance from&nbsp;
Lupita Nyong'o. The movie tells the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.920
story of a kindergarten teacher who has to&nbsp;
defend a group of children from an impending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:52.720
zombie apocalypse. As you might imagine, things&nbsp;
quickly get gory, messy, and bizarrely adorable.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:57.440
Obviously, there are a whole horde of zombie&nbsp;
movies out there, but Little Monsters manages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.240
to put a fresh spin on many of the genre's&nbsp;
most well-worn tropes. As a horror-comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.560
it can take certain tonal liberties other&nbsp;
films can't — and makes thorough use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.800
this opportunity, too. Moreover, Little&nbsp;
Monsters serves as definitive proof that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:16.480
Nyong'o is as skilled at comedy as she is at&nbsp;
drama, and acts as a delightful counterpoint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.680
to her more serious horror work. All in all,&nbsp;
this is the kind of offbeat, enchanting movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.400
that doesn't often get made in Hollywood these&nbsp;
days; especially in the world of zombie horror.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:32.400
Few things have had a bigger impact on American&nbsp;
culture than Barbie, and Tiny Shoulders:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:37.760
Rethinking Barbie explores the part that this&nbsp;
iconic toy has played in an ever-changing world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:42.480
Honing in on one specific item — especially&nbsp;
a toy — might have restricted a lesser work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.960
to shallowness, but Tiny Shoulders&nbsp;
uses this focus to deftly examine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:50.560
how western standards of beauty have&nbsp;
changed over the last few decades.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.040
Barbie has contributed to a number of shifts&nbsp;
in culture, but she's also followed them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.400
and often does both at the&nbsp;
same time. Suitably, then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:03.680
Tiny Shoulders follows Mattel as it prepares for&nbsp;
the doll's biggest reinvention ever. The movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:08.960
features interviews with a number of feminists,&nbsp;
media commentators, and even Mattel insiders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.000
and tackles the vital importance of Barbie's&nbsp;
role as a lightning rod in American culture.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.280
The Fyre Festival may have been the&nbsp;
absolute peak of 2010s scammer culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:24.640
and Fyre Fraud tells the full story in&nbsp;
excruciating detail. In case you didn't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:29.680
this 2017 festival promised to be one of&nbsp;
the most exclusive parties of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:35.040
Promotional material depicted the event as one&nbsp;
absolutely dripping in luxury — but when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.440
day of the festival actually arrived,&nbsp;
those promises failed to materialize.

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.680
"Now, total chaos has erupted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:46.240
Fans are fleeing the Fyre Festival on&nbsp;
emergency flights out of the Bahamas."

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:51.680
By focusing on Billy McFarland, the entrepreneur&nbsp;
at the center of the Fyre Festival plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:57.760
Fyre Fraud really gets to the rot at the heart of&nbsp;
the event. Specifically, it asks how he managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:02.880
convince the world that he knew what he was&nbsp;
doing, even when he really, really didn't.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.640
The documentary spends plenty of&nbsp;
time on the build-up to the festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:10.800
but it's definitely not time&nbsp;
wasted, as the payoff is almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:16.080
ludicrously sweet. The Fyre Festival scandal&nbsp;
was broadly publicized as it happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:20.880
but the documentary exposes the event's&nbsp;
horrible conditions in even closer detail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:25.920
Fyre Fraud also gets at something deeper about&nbsp;
the culture McFarland exploited, by suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.640
that people are most easily fooled when they're&nbsp;
offered something too glamorous to pass up.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.880
When you think of Kristen Stewart, you probably&nbsp;
don't think of quirky rom-coms — but if Happiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:42.560
Season is any indication, maybe you ought to.&nbsp;
This charming movie stars Stewart as Abby and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:46.880
Mackenzie Davis as Harper, a couple who&nbsp;
journey to Harper's family home for Christmas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:51.600
There's just one problem: Harper's straight-laced&nbsp;
parents don't know that their darling daughter is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:56.240
a lesbian. As a result, Abby has to pretend&nbsp;
that they're just close friends ... even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:59.920
though she's been eagerly planning to&nbsp;
pop the question on Christmas morning.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:04.400
Happiest Season plays into a number of&nbsp;
tropes found in the romantic comedy genre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:09.120
This doesn't drag the film down, however — in&nbsp;
fact, its strong central performances breathe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:15.360
new life into those old clichés. This movie&nbsp;
is a sweet and delightful festive diversion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.400
and serves a reminder that good&nbsp;
actors can do great work in any genre.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.920
Pete Davidson's turn toward the dramatic may&nbsp;
seem like a surprise, but for the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.520
the public seems to have embraced&nbsp;
it. He got a whole heap of positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:33.120
attention for The King of Staten Island,&nbsp;
and has now earned further praise for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:37.360
supporting turn in Big Time Adolescence.&nbsp;
This movie follows Davidson's Zeke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:41.280
a drug-dealing college dropout who&nbsp;
becomes a mentor to a rule-abiding teen.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.240
This is a classic coming-of-age story,&nbsp;
and certainly features a great deal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:51.440
the genre's typical conventions. Thanks to a&nbsp;
sharp script and a precise ear for dialogue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:56.240
however, Big Time Adolescence manages&nbsp;
to transcend its formula. Davidson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.840
in particular earned praise for his&nbsp;
performance in the film, which is a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:05.200
more sensitive turn than what people have come&nbsp;
to expect from him. The entire cast shines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:10.800
though, and they're a huge part of what makes&nbsp;
Big Time Adolescence one of Hulu's best films.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:16.640
Untouchable was one of the first documentaries&nbsp;
to tackle the rise and fall of Harvey Weinstein.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:20.400
Although the technical filmmaking&nbsp;
behind the movie is imperfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:24.000
Untouchable nonetheless earned praise&nbsp;
from critics, in part because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:29.760
urgency of the story that it tells. The movie&nbsp;
was released in 2018, a year after bombshell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:34.800
accounts of Weinstein's history of abuse began&nbsp;
to be published across major media platforms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:40.400
sparking the #MeToo movement. Viewers who&nbsp;
know those stories may not learn anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:45.520
new in Untouchable, but this doc does at least&nbsp;
bring them together in a much wider context.

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:50.720
As has become clear in the years since the&nbsp;
Weinstein story first broke, the now-reviled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:55.520
producer was far from the only Hollywood&nbsp;
personality to get away with horrendous abuses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:00.880
of power. Suitably, then, Untouchable examines not&nbsp;
just the allegations against Weinstein himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:04.640
but also the systems that enabled him&nbsp;
to hold onto his power for so long.

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:09.520
The stories following the creation of&nbsp;
comic book characters are often stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:14.560
of credit — about those who get it for bringing&nbsp;
such iconic characters to life, and those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:19.680
are ultimately brushed to the side. In Batman&nbsp;
&amp; Bill, audiences are treated to the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:24.800
Bill Finger, an unheralded comic book writer&nbsp;
who played a crucial role in creating Batman.

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:30.800
It wasn't until after Finger's death that people&nbsp;
began to recognize the massive contributions he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:34.880
made to the character. But this film&nbsp;
asks why Bob Kane instead got all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:39.200
credit for creating Batman, even though Finger&nbsp;
contributed the character's signature look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:44.080
his detective skills, and many of his&nbsp;
arch-nemeses, among other crucial details.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:48.640
"They would hire ghost writers back in the&nbsp;
day. Bob Kane would get credit for the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.560
he'd hand it in, 'here's my&nbsp;
Batman story,' they're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:53.840
'Bob, you're a genius,' and that's how the&nbsp;
industry worked for a long, long time."

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:58.880
Sadly, it took years for Finger's&nbsp;
legacy to be fully restored, but now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:03.600
at least, the world knows that Finger&nbsp;
played just as important a role as Kane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:07.400
if not more so — and this movie&nbsp;
is one of the reasons why.

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:14.880
Run is a taut, sleek, well-acted thriller.&nbsp;
Starring Sarah Paulson as a highly protective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:18.640
mother and Kiera Allen as her sick&nbsp;
daughter, the movie takes pains to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:24.240
establish their close relationship — before&nbsp;
things take several increasingly dark turns.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:28.960
At its heart, Run is a movie about being&nbsp;
imprisoned without even knowing it. It's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:33.680
about realizing the person you've spent your whole&nbsp;
life with isn't who they say they are. But while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:39.120
these thematic elements might feel weighty, the&nbsp;
movie itself is surprisingly light on its feet.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:44.800
Thanks to expert work from both Paulson and Allen,&nbsp;
these broadly drawn characters feel distinctly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:49.360
human. And even as the circumstances&nbsp;
of Run become increasingly absurd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:54.000
the relationship between the characters keeps&nbsp;
the story grounded with genuine emotional stakes.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:59.520
Lin-Manuel Miranda is one of the most prolific&nbsp;
creators working today. Thanks in part to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:04.000
success of Hamilton, he now has his hands&nbsp;
on a vast array of different projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:10.160
often acting, producing, writing, directing, or&nbsp;
doing any combination of those things. In We Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:15.840
Freestyle Love Supreme, Miranda helps to tell the&nbsp;
story of a group he started in 2004, years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:21.040
anyone knew who he was. The group was dedicated&nbsp;
to freestyle rap, and this documentary, directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:26.000
by Andrew Fried, tells their story while paying&nbsp;
tribute to the artform in which they specialized.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:29.200
Obviously, Miranda is an important&nbsp;
part of this story, but he's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:33.840
the sum total, and even those who might&nbsp;
be worn out by his peppy personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:37.760
will find plenty to love in We Are&nbsp;
Freestyle Love Supreme. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:42.000
freestyle rap is about creating something&nbsp;
beautiful in real time, and the scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:46.640
here that highlight that sense of spontaneity&nbsp;
make the documentary more than worth a watch.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:52.000
Although it shares a title with the famous&nbsp;
Dostoevsky novel, Crime + Punishment is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:56.640
a documentary firmly rooted in the American&nbsp;
experience. The film follows Latino and Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:01.360
police officers in the NYPD over the course of&nbsp;
four years, chronicling their brush-ups against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:05.520
the system and the ways in which policing is&nbsp;
biased toward people in their communities.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:10.640
Crime + Punishment doesn't come to any easy&nbsp;
answers about how the NYPD can be fixed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:15.200
Instead, it simply shines a light on the everyday&nbsp;
corruption that's become a part of policing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:19.360
and the other systemic abuses every police&nbsp;
officer eventually has to deal with.

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:24.240
Nor is Crime + Punishment a loud, shouty&nbsp;
movie. Instead, it makes its points quietly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:29.680
and precisely. Ultimately, though, that only&nbsp;
reinforces the movie's power, making it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:35.120
the more clear that the problems endemic to&nbsp;
policing aren't going anywhere. It's an urgent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:39.120
vital movie that gets at the humanity&nbsp;
of the police officers themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:43.280
even as it highlights the corruption of&nbsp;
the institution in which they operate.

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:47.440
Unlike many of the documentaries&nbsp;
on Hulu, the subject matter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:52.800
vibe of Ask Dr. Ruth is pretty easygoing. The&nbsp;
film chronicles the life of Dr. Ruth Westheimer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:56.640
a sex therapist who catapulted to fame&nbsp;
late in her life thanks to a TV show in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.760
which she provided candid advice&nbsp;
to callers about their sex lives.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:04.880
What makes Dr. Ruth remarkable is her utter&nbsp;
willingness to discuss sex in a way most Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:10.880
do not. She's not shy at all, and her frankness&nbsp;
about sex has arguably helped spur a new sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:16.720
of openness towards the subject. Thanks to her&nbsp;
slight stature, old age, and charming accent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:21.760
Dr. Ruth has also been able to reach a whole lot&nbsp;
of people who may have otherwise tuned her out.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:26.880
Ask Dr. Ruth is a deeply charming film, but it's&nbsp;
also one that encourages open conversation about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:31.920
topics that might otherwise be seen as taboo.&nbsp;
According to Dr. Ruth herself, sex should not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:36.640
be a topic we're afraid of: We should be&nbsp;
willing to dive in head first, she says, no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:41.920
matter what may come of it. And this documentary&nbsp;
embodies that stance with open-hearted abandon.

00:17:43.427 --> 00:17:47.200
Palm Springs debuted fairly early on&nbsp;
in the American COVID-19 lockdown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:50.240
and it couldn't have been more perfectly timed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:54.720
The comedy, which stars Andy Samberg and Cristin&nbsp;
Milioti, follows Milioti's character as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:59.440
becomes embroiled in a time loop that Samberg's&nbsp;
character has been stuck in for some time.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:02.960
"It's one of those infinite time loop&nbsp;
situations you might have heard about."

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:04.560
"That I might have heard about?"

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:05.060
"Yeah."

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:10.400
The movie has plenty of fun with that conceit,&nbsp;
but it also updates the Groundhog Day formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:15.360
by asking what would happen to a relationship in&nbsp;
which both people are forced to relive the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:20.480
day over and over again. Thanks to note-perfect&nbsp;
performances from Samberg and Milioti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:25.840
plus a little help from J.K. Simmons, Palm&nbsp;
Springs is always charming and frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:31.440
moving. As one of the very best movies that&nbsp;
Hulu has to offer, it delivers not just laughs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:35.360
but genuine emotion — and a&nbsp;
beautiful love story, to boot.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:40.720
With the advent of influencer culture, the&nbsp;
world has been treated to an entirely new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:43.840
type of celebrity, and for some&nbsp;
young people, the possibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:49.120
that the influencer lifestyle offers is&nbsp;
tantalizing. Jawline follows Austyn Tester,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:53.760
a teenager from Tennessee trying to make it big&nbsp;
online so he can escape his small-town life.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:57.440
Although there are plenty of movies out&nbsp;
there about the horrors of the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:02.480
Jawline is not one of them. Instead, it's a&nbsp;
tender look at one kid who's trying to make it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:08.000
and a subculture that has a tendency to consume&nbsp;
people like him and spit them out without ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:12.320
acknowledging their humanity. Jawline&nbsp;
doesn't pass any judgment on its subject,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:16.640
instead arguing that the idea of internet fame&nbsp;
is so attractive because it offers a route&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:21.360
out of the dead-end — something that seems&nbsp;
increasingly hard to find in today's world.

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:25.920
Sometimes, documentaries choose&nbsp;
to tell stories that most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:31.040
are already pretty familiar with. Other times,&nbsp;
though, they introduce brand new subjects to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:36.160
largely ignorant audience. So it goes in&nbsp;
Dumb: The Story of Big Brother Magazine.

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:39.680
Director Patrick O'Dell shines a&nbsp;
spotlight on this obscure publication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:45.280
which somehow catapulted a generation of skaters&nbsp;
to fame and also led to the creation of MTV's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:50.400
Jackass. Featuring interviews with a number&nbsp;
of the era's icons, this movie tells the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:55.840
of a generation of young rebels who eventually&nbsp;
became incredibly successful within their fields.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:59.760
Dumb isn't quite as widely known&nbsp;
as many of the films on this list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:04.960
but it's earned plenty of praise from critics,&nbsp;
who hailed the film as an effective chronicle of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:09.680
group of people who felt like breaking the rules…&nbsp;
for no other reason than because they could.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:14.720
Although it wasn't as much of a phenomenon&nbsp;
as the original March of the Penguins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:18.960
this sequel features all of the elements&nbsp;
that make the first film so winning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:23.760
Morgan Freeman returns to narrate the project,&nbsp;
and the story at the film's center is just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:28.560
compelling as anything that happens in the first&nbsp;
film. The Next Step follows a group of penguins as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:33.360
they journey to a mysterious destination after&nbsp;
one of their young hears an enigmatic call.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:38.400
Above all else, this sequel, which came&nbsp;
more than a decade after the original film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:43.360
serves as proof that penguins make deeply&nbsp;
compelling subjects for nature documentaries. Of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:48.400
course, even as the documentary wonders at their&nbsp;
beauty, it's hard not to see the impending threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:53.760
of climate change hanging over their heads. To&nbsp;
that end, March of the Penguins 2 isn't just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:59.360
the beauty of Antarctic wildlife — it's also a&nbsp;
harsh reminder that it may not be around forever.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:04.560
Too Funny to Fail is both hilarious&nbsp;
and refreshingly low-stakes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:08.400
The documentary tells the story of The Dana&nbsp;
Carvey Show — specifically, all the ways in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:13.280
which it failed to succeed, despite the many&nbsp;
talented people working behind the scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:16.800
Stephen Colbert, Steve Carell,&nbsp;
and, of course, Dana Carvey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:21.520
all worked on the show, but that didn't keep it&nbsp;
from flaming out in truly stupendous fashion.

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:26.320
In spite of the show's untimely demise, this&nbsp;
documentary remains a fairly lighthearted look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:31.200
what went wrong, including candid interviews with&nbsp;
all of these now-famous men. As it turns out, The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:36.240
Dana Carvey Show may have been ahead of its time,&nbsp;
and Too Funny to Fail demonstrates how well many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:40.960
of the show's sketches still hold up. And although&nbsp;
its failure might have been kinda depressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:46.880
at the time, the story here is told with a wry&nbsp;
smile — and the ever-useful benefit of hindsight.

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:52.640
Most magic shows tend to opt for spectacle&nbsp;
over meaning. With In &amp; of Itself, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:57.760
magician Derek DelGaudio steers hard into the&nbsp;
world of storytelling. Mixing amazing tricks with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:03.520
his own personal narrative, the show becomes an&nbsp;
experience that's both dazzling and deeply moving.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:07.840
In &amp; Of Itself is a filmed version of&nbsp;
DelGaudio's stage show of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:13.040
but it beautifully translates the experience to&nbsp;
screen. DelGaudio performs several tricks over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:17.680
course of the show, and none of them lose any of&nbsp;
their intense impact through the process of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:22.880
filmed. But though the tricks may be astounding,&nbsp;
they're also pretty deep: DelGaudio's show is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:28.400
examination of how we see ourselves, and how that&nbsp;
self-perception helps to guide the decisions we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:34.640
make. It's absolutely mesmerizing, and arguably&nbsp;
one of the best things on Hulu full stop.

